1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,000
This is a normal to-do list. For an ADHD, dyslexic, or anxious brain, it isn’t a plan — it’s a wall.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:13,500
Stepwise is our answer — built for the TaskForge hackathon, whose theme is accessible tools for focus and calm productivity.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The home screen refuses to show you a wall: your recent tasks, one input, and calm copy.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:25,000
Deciding can wait. Dump a task in exactly as it is — sorting it out is the app’s job, not yours.

5
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:32,000
Let’s add a real one: “Reply to Prof. Reyes about the thesis draft.”

6
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:39,500
Stepwise breaks it into small, concrete steps — with kind time estimates: a 35% buffer, because time blindness is real.

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Steps are chunked into sessions of at most four, with breaks in between. Every list stays short enough to breathe.

8
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:50,000
And the last step is always a reward. Finishing should feel like finishing.

9
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:56,500
Focus mode shows exactly one step. Everything else hides behind a peek — available, never pushed.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,500
The ring timer is quiet by design. Time is always shown as text — never color alone.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
It can be paused. And when time runs low, five kind minutes appear — no questions asked.

12
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:23,000
But you never have to wait for a timer. Press Done the moment it feels done.

13
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:30,000
The next step never auto-plays. You set the pace.

14
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:36,500
Every win gets a breath, not a slot machine.

15
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Or skip the step. Skipping is never failure — momentum matters more than perfection.

16
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,000
One more — and the session ends.

17
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,500
Sessions end with a built-in break, because rest is part of the work.

18
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:58,000
Back on the task, progress is quiet: no confetti pressure, no red badges.

19
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:05,500
This is the trail of wins: one stone per finished step, lanterns every fifth.

20
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Stats count steps, not streaks. There is no shame anywhere in this app.

21
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:20,500
Every stone is named and keyboard-focusable — screen readers get the same story.

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
Your data is yours: export or import as JSON, any time. No accounts, no cloud, no tracking.

23
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:35,000
The accessibility panel changes everything instantly — and remembers it, locally.

24
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:42,500
Five text sizes: the whole UI scales, not just paragraphs.

25
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,500
High contrast: black, white and gold, tested beyond AA.

26
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:56,500
Dyslexia-friendly text: rounder letters, wide spacing, a calmer rhythm.

27
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Reduce motion, for days when even gentle animation is too much.

28
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:11,000
And back to default in seconds. Your settings, your device.

29
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:18,500
Fully keyboard-driven: N for a new task, Space runs the timer, Esc always lets you out.

30
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,500
A polite live region announces every step change and timer milestone.

31
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
It’s a PWA: installable and fully offline. Your list works on a plane, or at 2 a.m. with no signal.

32
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:40,000
On a phone, the same calm: one column, big targets, nothing to hunt for.

33
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:47,500
Under the hood: readable rules, not AI. 24 passing tests, zero dependencies.

34
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Built during the hackathon, with the AI assistance disclosed — as the rules ask.

35
00:03:55,500 --> 00:04:03,000
Stepwise — one calm step at a time. The link is right below this video.
